  <p class="crumbs"><a href="/">Home</a> › <a href="/guides">Guides</a> › Preply alternatives</p>
  <h1>Preply alternatives: what to look for</h1>
  <p class="lede">Preply is a well-known online marketplace for 1-to-1 language lessons: learners browse tutor profiles, book a trial lesson, and then take regular lessons with a tutor they choose. If you are looking for a Preply alternative, the question is not which site is “best” — it is which one fits how you want to learn. This guide explains what Preply offers, what to check in any alternative, and how LearnNest compares.</p>

  <h2>What is Preply, in plain terms?</h2>
  <p>Preply is a marketplace that connects language learners with private tutors for live, one-to-one video lessons. Tutors set their own prices, and learners choose a tutor by language, price, availability and speciality. Preply says it has more than 100,000 tutors and offers lessons in over 90 languages.</p>
  <p>Lessons on Preply start with a trial lesson, which is paid and lasts 25 or 50 minutes. After the trial, learning continues through a subscription that renews every 28 days, with a minimum number of lessons per cycle. Lessons take place in Preply’s own online classroom, with a whiteboard and shared materials.</p>
  <h2>What to look for in any alternative</h2>
  <p>First, tutor choice: can you browse real profiles, read reviews, watch an introduction and choose your own tutor — or are you assigned one? Being able to choose matters, because the tutor is the most important part of the experience.</p>
  <p>Second, the trial: is there one, how long is it, and what does it cost? A trial lets you test a tutor’s style before you commit. Third, pricing: are prices clear per lesson, and do you know what happens after the trial — one-off lessons, or a subscription? Fourth, flexibility: can you reschedule, pause or change tutor easily? And fifth, the lesson itself: is there a proper online classroom, or do you need a separate video app?</p>
  <h2>Types of alternatives</h2>
  <p>There are several kinds of alternative. Other tutor marketplaces work the same way as Preply — you browse tutors and book 1-to-1 lessons — and differ mainly in their tutors, prices and rules. Language learning apps offer structured courses and exercises, which suit self-study but give you less real conversation. Local tutors and language schools offer face-to-face lessons, which some learners prefer. And free options like language exchange give you conversation practice with a partner, but no teacher and no structure.</p>
  <p>The right choice depends on your goal. If you want conversation with a real person, a tutor marketplace is the closest match. If you want to study alone at your own pace, an app may be enough. Many learners use more than one: an app for daily practice and a tutor for speaking.</p>
  <h2>How LearnNest compares</h2>
  <p>LearnNest is a demo tutor marketplace built to show how this kind of site works. It has 24 sample tutors in 12 languages, with prices from $8 to $45 per 50-minute lesson and a trial that costs half the lesson price. You can search, filter and compare sample profiles, and try a demo booking.</p>
  <p>The important difference is that LearnNest is a demo: the tutors are sample profiles, not real people, and no payment is taken and no lesson is actually booked. It is a way to see how a tutor marketplace works — not a service you can take lessons on. If you want real lessons, you need a live marketplace with real tutors, and this guide’s checklist above will help you judge one.</p>
  <h2>How to choose: a short checklist</h2>
  <p>Before you pay anywhere, ask these questions. Can I choose my own tutor from real profiles? Is there a trial, and what does it cost? What happens after the trial — subscription or pay-as-you-go? Can I reschedule or change tutor easily? Are prices clear, with no hidden fees? And do the reviews look real and recent?</p>
  <p>If a site cannot answer those questions clearly, be cautious. A good marketplace is open about its prices, its trial and its rules — because it wants you to stay, not to feel trapped.</p>
  <h2>Common mistakes to avoid</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>Choosing only on price — the cheapest tutor is not always the best fit for your goal.</li>
    <li>Skipping the trial and paying for a big package first.</li>
    <li>Ignoring the rules on rescheduling, pausing and changing tutor.</li>
    <li>Trusting a site just because it is well known — check the trial, prices and reviews yourself.</li>
  </ul>
  <h2>Practical tips</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>Try a trial lesson before you commit to anything.</li>
    <li>Compare at least three tutors for your language before choosing one.</li>
    <li>Read the pricing and subscription rules before you pay — especially what renews automatically.</li>
    <li>Use the checklist above on any marketplace, including Preply itself.</li>
  </ul>
